   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to requiring a written\nreport on plans and programs related to the prevention of suicide",
    "basePrintNo": "S6406",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6406-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §7.07, Ment Hyg L",
    "lawSection": "Mental Hygiene Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6406\n\nSPONSOR: SEPULVEDA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to\nrequiring a written report on plans and programs related to the\nprevention of suicide\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would require the Office of Mental Health to prepare a submit\na writer report on the development of plans, programs, and services to\nprevent and reduce suicide/suicidal behaviors, as well as a report on\nprevention services and program initiatives directed towards high risk\nminority groups and demographics.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Subdivision (g) of section 7.07 of the mental hygiene law, as\nadded by chapter 381 of the laws of 2018, is amended\n\nSection 2: This act shall take effect immediately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nWith a rise of mental health issues and mental health awareness, it is\nimportant for the public to be aware of the campaigns and efforts the\nOffice of Mental Health (OMB) are taking to prevent suicide and suicidal\nbehavior. Suicide is the 2nd leading cause of death for individual ages\n15-34. It is an ignored issue amongst high-risk minority communities who\nmay not necessary have the resources necessary to address the issues.\n\nThe OMH is already mandated to take steps in preventing such deaths.\nThis legislation will require a yearly report to be published regarding\nsuch steps, including a separate information on steps taken to address\nhigh risk minority groups or special populations, deemed as such by the\noffice. By having such report, we can see if enough work is being done\nand, if not, how to further address it.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nTo Be Decided\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately\n",
